Kawasaki Disease (Cardiac Complications)
Cardiovascular
Prolonged high fever >5 days, Mucocutaneous changes/Strawberry tongue, Desquamation of extremities, Coronary artery aneurysms on echo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-022
Reason for Visit: Persistent high fever for 6 days in a child, accompanied by red eyes, cracked lips, and sudden lethargy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Pediatric patient (Age 4) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a child with a prolonged, unrelenting fever and mucocutaneous changes (red eyes, cracked lips). This constellation strongly suggests Kawasaki Disease, which carries a critical risk of coronary artery aneurysms and acute myocardial infarction if untreated. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to identify these pathognomonic clinical signs and facilitate immediate hospital admission for intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy, precluding delay until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Parent: "He has had a burning fever for six days that Panadol won't touch, his eyes are bloodshot, and his lips are bleeding."
History of Present Illness: The 4-year-old patient has had a prolonged high fever (consistently >39.0 C) lasting 6 days. Parents note the development of bilateral bloodshot eyes without any sticky discharge. Yesterday, his lips became severely erythematous and cracked. He has a polymorphous generalized exanthem on his trunk. Parents also note he is refusing to walk, complaining his feet are swollen and sore. He appears unusually lethargic and irritable today, with a sudden episode of crying while clutching his chest an hour ago. KEY SYMPTOMS: Prolonged high fever lasting greater than 5 days. Bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection. Erythema and cracking of the lips. Sudden onset chest pain or signs of myocardial infarction in a child. Polymorphous generalized exanthem.
Functional Status: Lethargic, refusing to walk or eat.
Social/Safety Concerns: Parents are exhausted and highly distressed by the child's continuous decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 | HR: 145 | Temp: 39.4 C | RR: 28 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 16 Kg
General: Irritable, inconsolable pediatric patient, flushed and toxic appearance.
Physical Exam: HEENT: Bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection. Lips are deeply erythematous, fissured, and bleeding slightly. Prominent "strawberry tongue" with hypertrophied papillae. Unilateral painful right cervical lymphadenopathy (approx 2 cm). Skin/Extremities: Polymorphous macular rash on the trunk. Erythema and indurative edema of the hands and feet. Cardiovascular: Tachycardia out of proportion to the fever. A soft new systolic murmur is appreciated at the left sternal border. No obvious gallop. Lungs: Clear. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Prolonged high fever >5 days, Mucocutaneous changes/Strawberry tongue, Desquamation of extremities (late sign, currently in acute edema phase), Coronary artery aneurysms on echo (pending urgent inpatient echocardiogram).
Environmental Observations: Numerous empty paracetamol and ibuprofen bottles.
Medication Reconciliation: Alternating antipyretics with no sustained effect on the fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Kawasaki Disease (with suspected acute cardiac involvement) - Status: Critical (Acute Phase)
Differentials:

Scarlet Fever - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of non-exudative conjunctivitis and the specific extremity changes (edema/erythema), which are classic for Kawasaki rather than Group A Strep.

Viral Exanthem (e.g., Adenovirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prolonged nature of the fever (>5 days) unresponsive to antipyretics, combined with the strict tetrad of mucocutaneous and extremity signs, and the disproportionate tachycardia/new murmur.

Measles - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of Cough, Coryza, and Koplik spots; the child is fully vaccinated.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The child presents with the classic, complete clinical criteria for Kawasaki Disease: fever >5 days, non-exudative conjunctivitis, mucosal changes (strawberry tongue, cracked lips), polymorphous rash, extremity edema, and cervical lymphadenopathy. The sudden episode of chest clutching, combined with tachycardia out of proportion to fever and a new heart murmur, raises extreme concern for acute myocarditis or early coronary artery involvement. This is a pediatric medical emergency. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to confirm the diagnosis clinically and immediately arrange hospital transport, as treatment with IVIG must be initiated promptly to prevent permanent, fatal coronary artery aneurysms.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate referral to the Pediatric Emergency Department. Contacted the pediatric registrar on call to alert them of incoming suspected Kawasaki disease requiring urgent echocardiography and IVIG preparation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed parents to cease forcing oral fluids or food if the child resists, to avoid aspiration while highly distressed. By simply holding and comforting the child in a calm, cool environment, the parents reduce the child's metabolic demand and heart rate while awaiting transport. The reward is a calmer child and a safer transfer.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required. Long-term pediatric cardiology follow-up expected.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance transfer requested due to potential for rapid cardiac decompensation. If the child becomes completely unresponsive, turns blue, or stops breathing, parents are instructed to commence pediatric basic life support.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical but requires inpatient echocardiography to assess heart involvement; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Kawasaki disease is a severe inflammation of the blood vessels. Without immediate hospital treatment with specialized immune therapy, the child is at high risk of permanent heart damage.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not give any more over-the-counter medications. Wait for the ambulance. Keep the child as calm as possible.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your child has a severe inflammatory condition that affects the blood vessels, particularly those supplying the heart. The prolonged fever, red eyes, and cracked lips are the warning signs. He needs to go to the hospital right now for a special intravenous medicine that will stop the inflammation and protect his heart.
Actionable Steps: Pack a bag for the hospital. Let the paramedics handle the physical movement of the child to keep his heart rate down.

